codex
Implemented a self-contained Track B generator based on the paper’s native graph-isomorphism and permutation-group objects. It generates connected tree-width-3 projective wheel graphs with a planted 2×2 projective conjugacy witness. The paper’s polynomial/FPT algorithms are reported honestly as the mechanical route. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1803.06858v1)

Local verification passes all gates:

- Exact valid-answer density: \(2/(131071^2-1)\)
- Random guesses: 0/200,000
- Four attacks: 0/8 each
- Reference algorithm: 8/8, up to 3,151,396 operations
- Compact route: 65 operations
- Answer: 4 elements, 27 worst-case characters

Files:

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1803.06858/gen_1803_06858.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1803.06858/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1803.06858/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1803.06858/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1803.06858/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1803.06858/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The only blocker is external: every OpenRouter call returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded,” so all three oracle arms have zero scored attempts. The README and harness-owned transcripts record this honestly. The family is therefore locally verified but not yet shippable; STEP 4 and the G9 arms must be rerun after replenishing `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`.
