%We claim that~$u$ and~$u'$ cannot be separated by at most~$k-1$ vertices.
%\ipascal{Don't know how to proceed from here? Halls theorem?}
Let~$\gamma\in \Aut(G)$ be an element of order~$p$.
We argue that the set~$F_\gamma\coloneqq \fix(\gamma)$ is a clique.
Let~$v$ be a vertex not fixed by~$\gamma$, i.e., 
$v\nin F_\gamma$.
Consider~$C_v$, the connected component of~$G[V\setminus F_\gamma]$
containing~$v$.

We claim that~$N(C_v)\subseteq F_\gamma$ is a clique. Let $u, u'\in N(C_v)$
and
let~$P$ be a shortest path from~$u$ to~$u'$ such that all internal vertices of~$P$ are in~$C_v$. For~$i\in \mathbb{N}$ the paths~$P$ and~$P^{(\gamma^i)}$ are internally vertex disjoint since~$P$ is a shortest path and no vertex in~$P$ is fixed under~$\gamma^i$.
We conclude that there are at least~$p> k$ internally vertex disjoint paths
between~$u$ and~$u'$. Thus, since~$G$ is~$k$-improved,~$u$ and~$u'$ must be
adjacent. This proves the claim that $N(C_v)$ is clique.

Since $N(C_v)$ separates $F_\gamma\setminus N(C_v)$ from $v$ and $G$ has no
separating cliques, it follows that $N(C_v)=F_\gamma$. This proves
Assertion (1).



To prove (2), suppose that~$\gamma'\in \Aut(G)$ is a second element of
order~$p$ that commutes with~$\gamma$. Let~$F_{\gamma'}\coloneqq \fix(\gamma')$ be
the elements fixed by~$\gamma'$. By (1), $F_{\gamma'}$ is clique.
It suffices to show that every vertex~$u$ in~$F_\gamma\setminus F_{\gamma'}$ is adjacent to every vertex~$w$ in~$F_{\gamma'}\setminus F_{\gamma}$.

Since~$\gamma$ and~$\gamma'$ commute, the set~$F_{\gamma'}\setminus F_{\gamma}$ is invariant under~$\gamma$. Let~$P$ be a shortest path from~$u$ to~$w$. For~$i\in\mathbb{N}$, we know that~$P^{(\gamma^i)}$ is a path from~$u$ to~$w^{(\gamma^i)}\in F_{\gamma'}\setminus F_{\gamma}$.
The paths~$P,P^{ \gamma^1}, \ldots,P^{(\gamma^{p-1})}$ form a set
of~$p> k$ internally vertex disjoint path all ending in distinct
endpoints $w_0,\ldots,w_{p-1}$, respectively. We have $w_0$, and all
$w_i$ are adjacent to $w$, because they are elements of the clique
$F_{\gamma'}$. Hence there are $p$ mutually disjoint paths from $u$ to
$w$. As $G$ is $k$-improved, it follows
that~$u$ and~$w$ are adjacent.
